Do not set SO_REUSEADDR on Windows.
On Unix, SO_REUSEADDR lets a server bind its port again while old
connections are in TIME_WAIT. On Windows it lets a socket bind a port
that another socket is already listening on, and Windows then decides
which of them gets each connection. A second "nc -l -p 8080" or httpd
on a port in use started without an error, and another program could
take over connections to the port. Windows reuses addresses in
TIME_WAIT without the option.

Make setsockopt_reuseaddr() do nothing on Windows.

---
 libbb/xconnect.c |    6 ++++++
 1 file changed, 6 insertions(+)

--- a/libbb/xconnect.c
+++ b/libbb/xconnect.c
@@ -35,7 +35,13 @@
 
 void FAST_FUNC setsockopt_reuseaddr(int fd)
 {
+#if !ENABLE_PLATFORM_MINGW32
 	setsockopt_SOL_SOCKET_1(fd, SO_REUSEADDR);
+#else
+	/* Windows reuses an address in TIME_WAIT anyway, and SO_REUSEADDR
+	 * would let a socket bind a port that another one listens on. */
+	(void)fd;
+#endif
 }
 int FAST_FUNC setsockopt_broadcast(int fd)
 {
